ask(c,'Which description best matches a ball screw in the lecture?', ['Lower friction through recirculating balls and typically backdrivable','Guaranteed self-locking because balls prevent all motion','Higher friction because it has no rolling elements','No ability to carry axial load'], ['The rolling contact reduces friction and makes reverse motion under load possible in typical designs.','Typical backdrivability is the opposite of guaranteed self-locking.','Rolling balls are the defining feature.','Axial load transmission is the screw’s purpose.'])
ask(c,'Why should a designer not assume that the low-friction ball screw will hold a suspended load without a brake?', ['It may backdrive under the load','It necessarily has zero stiffness','It cannot convert motor rotation into translation','Its balls automatically disconnect the motor at rest'], ['Backdrivability permits output load to drive input rotation unless other provisions hold it.','Low friction does not imply zero stiffness.','Rotary-linear conversion remains its function.','The source describes no automatic disconnection.'],kind='scenario',difficulty='medium')
c=concept(589,'Compliance, pose error and resonance','Rigid-model limitations, gravity sag and control-bandwidth constraints.')
ask(c,'Why can accurate joint-position readings still lead to an inaccurate tool-position estimate under load?', ['Unmodeled link or transmission deformation violates the rigid forward-kinematics assumptions','Forward kinematics never uses joint positions','Gravity cannot affect a robot with sensors','Accurate encoders automatically measure every link’s elastic deflection'], ['A rigid model maps joint angles to undeformed geometry, not necessarily the loaded tool pose.','The slide explicitly uses sensed joints for forward kinematics.','Gravity can deform compliant structures.','A joint encoder does not automatically measure all downstream bending.'],difficulty='medium')
ask(c,'Which dynamic consequence of flexibility is emphasized?', ['Mechanical resonances that can limit control bandwidth','Guaranteed elimination of all vibration','Infinite stiffness at every drive frequency','Automatic cancellation of every external load'], ['Elastic elements and inertia can produce resonant response.','Flexibility can introduce vibration rather than eliminate it.','Flexibility is not infinite stiffness.','External loading is not canceled simply by elasticity.'])
persist()
# Stiffness equations and physically distinct calculation angles.
c=concept(590,'Equivalent stiffness and compliance','Parallel and series rules, limiting behavior and common-coordinate referral.',[590,592])
SPR=fm('k_parallel=k1+k2; 1/k_series=1/k1+1/k2; C=1/k',{'k1,k2':'positive component stiffnesses referred to the same coordinate','k_parallel,k_series':'equivalent stiffness','C':'compliance','k':'stiffness of the element under discussion'},{'k1,k2,k_parallel,k_series,k':'N/m for translation, or N·m/rad for rotation','C':'m/N for translation, or rad/(N·m) for rotation'},['Linear scalar springs; parallel elements share displacement; series elements carry the same generalized force; use one consistent coordinate and unit system.'])
ask(c,'Why do stiffnesses add for two parallel linear springs?', ['They undergo the same displacement and their forces add','They carry the same force and their displacements add','Their deflections must be opposite and cancel','Their lengths must be equal regardless of loading'], ['F_total=k1δ+k2δ=(k1+k2)δ.','That describes springs in series.','Ordinary parallel loading shares the same displacement.','The stiffness rule follows compatibility, not a universal equal-length requirement.'],pages=[590],formula=[SPR])
ask(c,'Two translational springs of 100 and 300 N/m are connected in series. What is the equivalent stiffness?', ['75 N/m','400 N/m','200 N/m','1/400 N/m'], ['k=100×300/(100+300)=75 N/m.','400 is the parallel stiffness.','The arithmetic mean is not the series rule.','This is neither the reciprocal-compliance sum nor the resulting stiffness.'],kind='numerical',pages=[590],formula=[SPR],steps=['C_total=1/100+1/300=1/75 m/N.','Take the reciprocal to obtain 75 N/m.'])
ask(c,'The same 100 and 300 N/m springs are instead in parallel under a total force of 40 N. What is their common displacement?', ['0.1 m','40/75 m','0.4 m','1.6 m'], ['k_total=400 N/m, so δ=40/400=0.1 m.','This uses the series equivalent stiffness.','This ignores the 300 N/m spring.','This multiplies unrelated quantities rather than dividing force by stiffness.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',pages=[590],formula=[SPR,fm('δ=F/k',{'δ':'linear displacement','F':'applied force','k':'equivalent translational stiffness'},{'δ':'m','F':'N','k':'N/m'},['Small linear elastic deflection.'])],steps=['Parallel stiffness is 100+300=400 N/m.','Use F=kδ to find δ=40/400=0.1 m.'])
ask(c,'For two finite positive stiffnesses in series, which relationship holds?', ['The equivalent stiffness is lower than either component stiffness','The equivalent stiffness exceeds their sum','The equivalent stiffness equals their arithmetic mean','The equivalent stiffness is their product without a denominator'], ['Adding positive compliances makes total compliance larger than either contribution.','Series connection makes the system more compliant.','The series formula is harmonic, not arithmetic averaging.','A bare stiffness product also has incorrect dimensions.'],pages=[590],formula=[SPR])
ask(c,'A motor-side stiffness is 100 N·m/rad, followed by an ideal η=3 reducer and an output-side spring of 900 N·m/rad in series. What is the total output-referred stiffness?', ['450 N·m/rad','90 N·m/rad','1800 N·m/rad','1000 N·m/rad'], ['Refer the input stiffness to output: 3²×100=900. Two 900 stiffnesses in series give 450.','90 results from combining untransformed 100 with 900.','1800 is the parallel combination after referral.','1000 adds the unmatched raw stiffnesses.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',formula=[SPR,fm('k_o=η²k_i',{'k_i':'input-referred rotational stiffness','k_o':'output-referred equivalent stiffness','η':'ideal input/output speed ratio'},{'k_i,k_o':'N·m/rad','η':'dimensionless'},['Ideal rigid ratio transformation; other elastic stages are combined separately.'])],steps=['Transform the motor spring: k_o=9×100=900 N·m/rad.','Add output-referred compliances: 1/k_total=1/900+1/900.','Thus k_total=450 N·m/rad.'],assumptions=['The reducer itself is ideal and rigid; both elastic stages are linear.'])
issue(590,'common-coordinate','Reduction stages are described as series compliances without stating that they must first be referred to a common shaft.','Apply p592’s η² stiffness transformation before combining rotational compliances; raw values from different coordinates cannot simply be added.')
c=concept(591,'Shaft and gear torsional stiffness','Solid shaft geometry, material properties, units and output gear approximation.')
SHAFT=fm('k=Gπd⁴/(32l)',{'k':'torsional stiffness, torque per twist angle','G':'shear modulus','d':'solid shaft diameter','l':'shaft length'},{'k':'N·m/rad','G':'Pa','d,l':'m'},['Uniform solid circular shaft; linear elastic small torsional deformation; G,d,l>0.'])
GG=fm('k=C_g b r²',{'k':'output-gear torsional stiffness with input fixed','C_g':'material/contact-model coefficient','b':'gear face width','r':'output gear radius'},{'k':'N·m/rad','C_g':'N/m² with radians treated as dimensionless','b,r':'m'},['Use the slide’s approximation and a fixed input gear; C_g remains unchanged in scaling comparisons.'])
ask(c,'Which geometric quantity has fourth-power influence on the solid shaft’s torsional stiffness?', ['Diameter d','Length l','Shear modulus G','Applied torque τ'], ['The printed formula contains d⁴.','Length appears in the denominator to the first power.','G appears linearly.','Applied torque determines twist for a given linear stiffness but is not a geometric factor in the formula.'],formula=[SHAFT])
ask(c,'If shaft diameter doubles while material and length remain unchanged, how does torsional stiffness change?', ['It increases by a factor of 16','It increases by a factor of 2','It increases by a factor of 4','It decreases by a factor of 16'], ['(2d)⁴/d⁴=16.','This ignores the fourth power.','This would follow a square-law dependence.','A larger diameter increases stiffness in this model.'],kind='numerical',formula=[SHAFT],steps=['Take the ratio k_new/k_old=(2d)⁴/d⁴.','Evaluate 2⁴=16.'])
ask(c,'If shaft length doubles with all other properties unchanged, what happens to stiffness and twist under the same torque?', ['Stiffness halves and twist doubles','Stiffness doubles and twist halves','Both stiffness and twist halve','Both remain unchanged'], ['k∝1/l and twist φ=τ/k, so the effects are reciprocal.','This reverses the length dependence.','A smaller stiffness increases, not reduces, twist under fixed torque.','Length directly appears in the stiffness formula.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[SHAFT],assumptions=['Same applied torque; linear torsional response.'])
ask(c,'For G=80 GPa, d=0.02 m and l=0.5 m, what is the solid shaft stiffness?', ['800π N·m/rad, approximately 2513.27 N·m/rad','80π N·m/rad, approximately 251.33 N·m/rad','800π N/m, approximately 2513.27 N/m','1600π N·m/rad, approximately 5026.55 N·m/rad'], ['80×10⁹×(0.02)⁴/(32×0.5)=800 before multiplying by π.','This is a factor-ten unit/power error.','The formula is torsional, not translational stiffness.','This doubles the correctly evaluated result.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',formula=[SHAFT],steps=['Convert 80 GPa to 80×10⁹ N/m².','d⁴=(0.02)⁴=1.6×10⁻⁷ m⁴.','Gd⁴=12800 N·m²; 32l=16 m.','k=(12800/16)π=800π N·m/rad.'])
ask(c,'For the output-gear stiffness approximation k=C_g b r², what happens if b doubles and r is halved with C_g unchanged?', ['Stiffness becomes one-half of its original value','Stiffness stays unchanged','Stiffness doubles','Stiffness becomes one-quarter of its original value'], ['The factor is 2×(1/2)²=1/2.','The radius is squared, so its halving is not canceled by doubling width.','This ignores the radius change.','This ignores the width doubling.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',formula=[GG],steps=['Form the ratio (2b)(r/2)²/(br²).','Simplify to 2/4=1/2.'])
ask(c,'What boundary condition accompanies the printed output-gear stiffness estimate?', ['The input gear is fixed','The output gear is fixed and its angle cannot be measured','Both gears spin freely without resistance','Both gears are removed from contact'], ['The output twist is assessed with the input held fixed.','The output is the compliant side under assessment.','A freely moving input changes the effective stiffness problem.','The formula describes engaged gear compliance.'],formula=[GG])
c=concept(592,'Reflected, belt and beam stiffness','Different stiffness types, gear-square transformation and axial/bending geometry.')
BELT=fm('k=AE/l',{'k':'axial belt stiffness','A':'cross-sectional area','E':'Young’s modulus','l':'effective tensile length'},{'k':'N/m','A':'m²','E':'Pa','l':'m'},['Uniform linearly elastic belt under tension; fixed material/area for length scaling.'])
ROUND=fm('k=3πE(d_o⁴−d_i⁴)/(64l³)',{'k':'transverse tip-force/tip-deflection stiffness','E':'Young’s modulus','d_o,d_i':'outer and inner diameters','l':'cantilever length'},{'k':'N/m','E':'Pa','d_o,d_i,l':'m'},['Uniform hollow circular cantilever, fixed at one end and transverse point load at the free end; small linear elastic bending; d_o>d_i≥0 and l>0.'])
SQUARE=fm('k=E(w_o⁴−w_i⁴)/(4l³)',{'k':'transverse tip stiffness','E':'Young’s modulus','w_o,w_i':'outer and inner square-section widths','l':'cantilever length'},{'k':'N/m','E':'Pa','w_o,w_i,l':'m'},['Uniform hollow square cantilever under small transverse end-load bending; w_o>w_i≥0 and l>0.'])
ask(c,'Why does ideal gear reduction transform input rotational stiffness by η² rather than only η?', ['Torque is multiplied by η and output angular deflection is divided by η','Only the torque changes, while the angular deflection is identical','Both torque and deflection are multiplied by η','The material modulus is physically multiplied by η²'], ['k_o=τ_o/δθ_o=(ητ_i)/(δθ_i/η)=η²k_i.','Ignoring the displacement transformation loses one ratio factor.','That would leave stiffness unchanged, contrary to the stated kinematics.','The effect is coordinate transformation, not a change in material properties.'],difficulty='medium',pages=[586,592],steps=['Use the ideal torque and angle-ratio relations.','Divide transformed torque by transformed angular displacement.','The two factors of η multiply.'],assumptions=['Ideal lossless fixed-ratio transmission, small elastic input displacement.'])
ask(c,'For A=2 mm², E=1 GPa and l=0.5 m, what axial belt stiffness follows?', ['4000 N/m','4 N/m','4×10⁹ N/m','1000 N/m'], ['A=2×10⁻⁶ m² and E=10⁹ Pa, so AE/l=2000/0.5=4000.','This is a factor-thousand conversion error.','This treats square millimetres as square metres.','This multiplies by length rather than dividing.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',formula=[BELT],steps=['Convert area to 2×10⁻⁶ m².','Compute AE=2000 N.','Divide by 0.5 m to get 4000 N/m.'])
ask(c,'Which pair correctly distinguishes the two stiffnesses k_o=η²k_i and k=AE/l?', ['Rotational stiffness in N·m/rad; axial stiffness in N/m','Both are axial stiffnesses in N/m','Both are mass moments in kg·m²','Rotational stiffness in N/m; axial stiffness in N·m/rad'], ['The former relates torque and angle; the latter relates force and extension.','The gear-referral equation is for the rotational drive.','Stiffness is not inertia.','The physical unit assignments are reversed.'])
ask(c,'If the inner diameter of a hollow round beam approaches its fixed outer diameter, what does the printed bending stiffness approach?', ['Zero','Infinity','The solid beam stiffness','A negative value'], ['The section term d_o⁴−d_i⁴ tends to zero as the wall vanishes.','There is no vanishing denominator in this limit.','A solid beam corresponds to d_i=0, not d_i=d_o.','Within the valid geometric range the difference is nonnegative.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[ROUND])
ask(c,'For either listed hollow cantilever, doubling length while keeping material and cross section fixed changes transverse stiffness by what factor?', ['1/8','1/2','1/4','8'], ['The denominator contains l³.','This is the shaft-torsion or belt-axial length scaling, not beam bending.','A squared-length scaling is not printed here.','Increasing length reduces bending stiffness.'],kind='numerical',formula=[ROUND,SQUARE],steps=['k_new/k_old=l³/(2l)³=1/8.'])
ask(c,'A hollow round beam has d_o=2d_i. What fraction of the stiffness of a solid beam with the same outer diameter, material and length is retained?', ['15/16','3/4','1/2','1/16'], ['The ratio is 1−(d_i/d_o)⁴=1−1/16=15/16.','This applies a square rather than fourth power.','This uses a linear diameter ratio.','1/16 is the removed fraction, not the retained fraction.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',formula=[ROUND],steps=['Divide the hollow stiffness by the solid expression with d_i=0.','Obtain 1−(d_i/d_o)⁴.','Insert d_i/d_o=1/2 to get 15/16.'])
ask(c,'For a hollow square beam, which dimensions enter the section term?', ['Outer width to the fourth power minus inner width to the fourth power','Outer width squared minus inner width squared','Outer width minus inner width only','Outer width to the fourth power plus inner width to the fourth power'], ['The supplied formula uses w_o⁴−w_i⁴.','The fourth power reflects bending geometry, not cross-sectional area alone.','Wall thickness alone does not capture the stated second moment.','Removing interior material reduces, not increases, the section term.'],formula=[SQUARE])
ask(c,'Which boundary/loading condition is needed for the two stated cantilever stiffness formulas?', ['One end clamped and a small transverse point load at the free end','Both ends pinned and a central point load','A uniform axial load with both ends sliding freely','A pure torsional couple on a solid shaft'], ['The slide models the link as a cantilever under an end load.','That beam support/load case has a different deflection coefficient.','The formulas are bending rather than axial stiffness.','Torsion uses G and a polar section moment, as on p591.'],kind='diagram',formula=[ROUND,SQUARE])
persist()
# Motors, position feedback and force sensors.
c=concept(593,'Electric and brushed DC actuation','Precision control, commutation, thermal limits and torque-current relation.')
ask(c,'Which advantages of electric actuation are emphasized in the lecture?', ['Easy interfacing and precise control','No winding heating and unlimited continuous torque','No need for any electrical supply','Guaranteed highest power-to-weight ratio for every application'], ['These are the stated reasons electric motors suit precision robotics.','The slide identifies winding overheating as a limit.','Electric motors require an electrical energy source.','The source does not make that universal maximum claim.'])
ask(c,'How does the described brushed DC motor deliver current to its rotor windings?', ['Stationary brushes contact a revolving commutator','A stationary commutator contacts revolving brushes attached to the rotor','Recirculating mechanical balls carry current through the screw threads','Only permanent magnets supply winding current with no circuit'], ['This is the physical contact arrangement stated on the slide.','It reverses the stationary and rotating parts.','Ball screws are mechanical transmissions, not the described commutation system.','Magnets do not supply electrical winding current without a circuit.'])
ask(c,'Which factor does the lecture identify as a primary limitation on the brushed motor’s operation?', ['Overheating of the windings','The joint count always exceeding six','The absence of any torque-current relationship','A mandatory zero shaft speed at every nonzero current'], ['Electrical losses can heat windings and limit sustained operation.','Mechanism joint count is unrelated to this stated thermal limit.','The slide explicitly gives a torque-current proportionality.','A motor can rotate while producing torque.'])
MOTOR=fm('τ=K_t I',{'τ':'motor torque','K_t':'torque constant','I':'armature current'},{'τ':'N·m','K_t':'N·m/A','I':'A'},['Constant flux and linear operating range; K_t is provided or calibrated.'])
ask(c,'A motor with K_t=0.4 N·m/A operates in its linear constant-flux range at 3 A. What torque does the stated proportional model predict?', ['1.2 N·m','7.5 N·m','0.133 N·m','3.4 N·m'], ['τ=K_tI=0.4×3=1.2.','This divides current by the torque constant.','This divides the constant by current.','The model is multiplicative, not additive.'],kind='numerical',formula=[MOTOR],steps=['Use the supplied constant-flux torque-current relation.','Multiply 0.4 N·m/A by 3 A to obtain 1.2 N·m.'])
c=concept(594,'Servo feedback and co-location','Position-error direction, effort commands and actuator-side sensing limits.',[594,589])
ask(c,'What signal does the described position servo use to form an actuator force or torque command?', ['Desired joint position minus measured joint position','Measured joint position plus desired joint position only','Only the total number of robot links','Only the output gear diameter, independent of error'], ['The control law uses position error as its feedback input.','A sum is not the stated error definition.','Link count alone is not the measured tracking error.','Gear size does not replace feedback error.'],pages=[594])
ask(c,'A desired joint angle is 30° and the measured angle is 26°. Under the slide’s error convention, what is the position error?', ['+4°','−4°','56°','4°/s'], ['Desired minus measured gives 30−26=4°.','This uses the reversed subtraction.','This adds rather than subtracts.','The error is a position difference, not a velocity.'],kind='numerical',pages=[594],steps=['Use e=θ_desired−θ_measured.','Compute e=30°−26°=+4°.'],formula=[fm('e=θ_d−θ_m',{'e':'joint position error','θ_d':'desired joint position','θ_m':'measured joint position'},{'e,θ_d,θ_m':'deg'},['The question uses the desired-minus-measured convention.'])])
ask(c,'What sensor placement is called co-located in this lecture?', ['The position sensor directly on the actuator shaft','The sensor only at a remote environmental landmark','The sensor at the tool while the actuator is on a different shaft','A sensor with no physical relation to the actuator coordinate'], ['The sensor observes the actuator’s own coordinate.','That is not the described co-location.','This is a spatially separated measurement, not the stated shaft pairing.','Co-location has a specific mechanical placement meaning here.'],pages=[594])
ask(c,'Does an actuator-shaft encoder by itself guarantee accurate loaded tool position through a flexible transmission?', ['No; downstream deflection may not be measured by that encoder','Yes; co-location makes every downstream element rigid','Yes; the shaft angle uniquely measures all link strain','No; co-located sensing cannot ever be used in feedback'], ['Co-location can support feedback design without measuring all downstream elastic deformation.','Sensor placement does not change material stiffness.','One shaft coordinate does not observe every deformation mode.','The source recommends co-location for a servo; the limitation is not total uselessness.'],difficulty='medium',conflict=(594,'Co-located sensor and actuator pairs are the most effective design strategy.','This is a design recommendation, not a universal loaded tool-pose accuracy guarantee. p589 explicitly identifies unmodeled compliance as a source of tool positioning error.'))
issue(594,'colocation-scope','The unqualified most-effective co-location recommendation can be mistaken for guaranteed tool-pose accuracy.','Distinguish actuator-coordinate feedback from downstream elastic displacement and use p589 as the explicit qualification.')
c=concept(595,'Strain-gauge force sensing','Bonded flexures, placement, wrench components and visible gauge structure.')
ask(c,'What does the bonded strain gauge directly respond to in the described force sensor?', ['Strain of the metal structure to which it is bonded','The robot’s absolute x coordinate without deformation','Only the commanded motor voltage','Only the number of ball bearings in the wrist'], ['Force is inferred through the calibrated relation between structural strain and load.','A strain gauge is not an absolute position tracker.','Its mechanical strain response is distinct from a motor command.','Bearing count is not the measured strain signal.'])
ask(c,'Which locations are listed for force sensing?', ['Joint actuators, wrist joints and end-effector fingertips','Only the fixed foundation, with no other allowed location','Only camera lenses and wheel hubs','Only the robot’s external control cabinet'], ['These are the three source-listed placement categories.','The source names several placements.','Those are not the listed set.','A cabinet-only location would not match the given examples.'])
ask(c,'A full six-component wrist wrench measurement includes which quantities?', ['Three force components and three moment components','Six independent position coordinates only','Six force magnitudes with no directions or moments','Three velocities and three accelerations'], ['A spatial wrench combines force and torque/moment vectors.','Pose coordinates are not force-torque components.','The six components include moments, not six scalar force magnitudes.','Those are kinematic rather than load quantities.'],assumptions=['A six-axis wrist sensor, not every device in the source’s 3–6 component range.'])
ask(c,'What does the enlarged inset in the force-sensor image show?', ['A bonded serpentine strain-gauge pattern with electrical leads','A ball-screw recirculation path','A six-leg Stewart linkage','An optical encoder disk with angular graduations'], ['The close-up shows the gauge grid and two connecting leads on the metal surface.','There are no recirculating balls in this inset.','The pictured element is a sensor grid, not a parallel manipulator.','No graduated rotating disk is shown.'],kind='diagram')
ask(c,'Which design set is explicitly relevant to the strain-gauge force sensor?', ['Gauge count and placement, stiffness, sensitivity and overload protection','Only outer color and cable length','Only maximum motor speed and reducer ratio','Only link count and inverse-kinematics branch count'], ['The slide names these sensing design factors.','These do not cover the mechanical sensing and protection requirements.','Actuator sizing alone does not determine sensor quality.','Kinematic counts are not the listed sensor design set.'])
c=concept(596,'Flexure sensor tradeoffs and protection','Stiffness-sensitivity tradeoff, overload stops, hysteresis, differential rejection and gauge types.')
ask(c,'With the same displacement readout and applied load, why does increasing flexure stiffness reduce deflection sensitivity?', ['The displacement per unit force is 1/k','The displacement per unit force is k','The force becomes independent of every deformation','The readout necessarily changes its units to torque'], ['For F=kδ, δ/F=1/k, so larger k produces smaller deflection per force.','This inverts the force-displacement relation.','The elastic measurement relies on deformation.','Stiffness change does not automatically change the measured physical quantity.'],difficulty='medium',assumptions=['Linear scalar flexure and otherwise unchanged measurement gain.'])
ask(c,'How do mechanical limit stops protect a flexure sensor from overload?', ['They limit excessive flexure deformation','They make the flexure infinitely sensitive','They remove every need for calibration','They increase the permitted deformation without bound'], ['A stop engages before damaging travel is exceeded.','Protection does not imply infinite sensing gain.','Calibration remains necessary within the usable range.','Limiting, not increasing, deformation is the mechanism.'])
ask(c,'How should minimizing welded or bolted joints near flexures be interpreted with respect to hysteresis?', ['As a way to reduce a source of hysteresis, not proof that all hysteresis is eliminated','As a guarantee that material and electronics can have no hysteresis','As a requirement to maximize all nearby mechanical interfaces','As a method that necessarily removes the sensor’s strain response'], ['Other mechanical/material/electronic sources may remain after reducing problematic interfaces.','Removing one source cannot prove that every source is absent.','The source recommends minimizing these interfaces.','The desired elastic strain response should remain.'],difficulty='medium',conflict=(596,'Eliminating hysteresis is achieved by minimizing welded or bolted joints near flexures.','Reducing such interfaces can reduce hysteresis mechanisms; it does not logically guarantee zero total hysteresis from all remaining materials and components.'))
ask(c,'Two sensor channels are y1=s+b and y2=−s+b, where s is the desired load signal and b the same temperature bias. What does (y1−y2)/2 produce?', ['s','b','s+b','0 for every load'], ['The common bias cancels and the opposite signal contributions add.','That would be obtained from their average, not half-difference.','The common bias is removed by subtraction.','Opposite signal signs yield 2s, not zero.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',steps=['Subtract: y1−y2=(s+b)−(−s+b)=2s.','Divide by two to recover s.'],formula=[fm('(y1−y2)/2=s',{'y1,y2':'paired sensor outputs','s':'opposite-sign desired response','b':'equal common-mode bias'},{'y1,y2,s,b':'same calibrated output unit'},['The two channels have matched bias and gain as explicitly specified; arbitrary differential pairs need not cancel disturbances.'])])
ask(c,'Which foil-versus-semiconductor gauge tradeoff is given?', ['Foil gauges are more durable but less sensitive; semiconductor gauges are more sensitive but more overload-vulnerable','Foil gauges are always more sensitive and more fragile','Semiconductor gauges cannot respond to strain','Both types have identical sensitivity and overload behavior'], ['This is the lecture’s qualitative comparison.','It reverses the stated tradeoff.','Semiconductor gauges are explicitly used for their strain sensitivity.','The source contrasts their behavior.'])
issue(596,'hysteresis-guarantee','Minimizing welded/bolted joints is worded as eliminating hysteresis.','Ask about reducing a contributing source, not proving zero hysteresis. Differential cancellation is tested only under explicit matched-channel assumptions.')
