c=concept(599,'Wheeled efficiency and static stability','Flat-ground benefits, support geometry, payload effects and nonuniversal balance assumptions.')
ask(c,'Which operating condition is explicitly linked to efficient wheeled locomotion in the lecture?', ['Flat ground with relatively simple rolling mechanics','Any terrain with no traction requirement','Only free flight without ground contact','Only vertical walls regardless of wheel design'], ['The source’s efficiency statement is explicitly tied to flat ground.','Traction and terrain still matter.','Wheels are being discussed as ground-contact locomotion.','Wall climbing requires other design provisions not implied here.'])
ask(c,'What must be checked before treating three wheels as sufficient for static stability under gravity?', ['Noncollinear support contacts and the COM projection lying inside their support triangle','Only that the three wheel diameters are equal','Only that the robot has three independent motor currents','Only that the body’s planar pose has three coordinates'], ['Three suitably arranged contacts can support a load only when the gravity line is supported by their polygon.','Equal diameters do not determine the loaded COM position.','Actuator count does not establish passive static support.','Pose dimension is not a stability condition.'],difficulty='medium',assumptions=['Level rigid ground, stationary robot, adequate friction, no additional overturning loads; COM denotes the combined robot/payload center of mass.'],conflict=(599,'Three wheels are typically sufficient for static stability.','The usual statement presumes a nondegenerate support polygon and a gravity line inside it. Wheel count alone does not prove stability.'))
ask(c,'A payload shifts the combined COM projection outside the wheel-contact support polygon while the robot is stationary. What follows under the stated static support criterion?', ['Static stability is lost even though the number of wheels is unchanged','Static stability is guaranteed by having at least three wheels','The planar pose loses its orientation coordinate','The wheel radius alone determines the outcome, independent of COM'], ['Support geometry and load placement, not wheel count alone, determine whether gravity can be balanced without tipping.','A support-count rule cannot override the supplied COM condition.','The pose representation remains (x,y,θ).','The stated criterion explicitly depends on the COM projection.'],kind='scenario',difficulty='medium',assumptions=['On level ground without attachments or external stabilizing supports, static stability requires the combined COM’s vertical projection inside the support polygon.'])
issue(599,'static-support','Three wheels and generally maintained contact can be misread as unconditional stability guarantees.','State level-ground, noncollinear-contact and COM-support conditions; keep traction, dynamic loads and unusual wheel layouts distinct.')
c=concept(600,'Wheel geometry and application tradeoffs','Wheel type/arrangement and joint consideration of maneuverability, stability and controllability.')
