ask(c,'How should a designer respond to the lecture’s “no ideal configuration” statement?', ['Choose a drive layout for the application’s terrain, stability, maneuverability and control constraints','Use the same wheel arrangement for every application because geometry cannot matter','Optimize maneuverability alone and assume stability follows automatically','Choose maximum wheel count and assume all tradeoffs disappear'], ['The choice is a task-dependent design tradeoff among the listed characteristics.','The source explicitly says geometry affects performance.','The characteristics are not interchangeable guarantees.','Wheel count alone cannot remove all constraints.'])
c=concept(601,'Planar chassis pose and workspace','Three pose coordinates, internal-joint distinction and configuration versus instantaneous mobility.')
ask(c,'Which vector represents a rigid chassis pose on a horizontal plane?', ['(x,y,θ)','(x,y,z,roll,pitch,yaw) with all six free','(wheel1 angle,wheel2 angle) only','(force_x,force_y,torque_z)'], ['Two global position coordinates and one heading describe planar pose.','A chassis constrained to a plane does not have all six independent spatial pose freedoms.','Wheel angles do not by themselves name the chassis pose in the global frame.','That is a planar wrench, not a pose.'])
ask(c,'A mobile chassis has four wheels and several internal actuators. How many coordinates still describe its planar rigid-body pose?', ['Three','Four, one for each wheel','One for every internal actuator','Six because every rigid body must move spatially'], ['Internal mechanisms do not increase the three-coordinate planar chassis pose.','Wheel count does not set pose dimension.','Internal joint coordinates and chassis pose are different state components.','The planar constraint removes three spatial freedoms.'])
ask(c,'A wheeled chassis has pose (x,y,θ), but an explicitly imposed no-side-slip condition prevents direct sideways velocity. Does the three-coordinate pose representation become invalid?', ['No; configuration coordinates and independently available instantaneous velocities are different notions','Yes; remove y from the pose because it can never change through any maneuver','Yes; heading is no longer part of the pose','No; three pose coordinates prove arbitrary sideways velocity is always possible'], ['A velocity constraint can restrict instantaneous motion without removing a coordinate from the pose description.','A constrained vehicle can change global y through suitable forward/turning motions.','Heading remains necessary to specify planar orientation.','Coordinate dimension alone does not guarantee an arbitrary instantaneous velocity vector.'],difficulty='medium',assumptions=['The no-side-slip restriction is an additional explicit model assumption in this question; the slide itself supplies the planar pose coordinates.'])
ask(c,'What does the mobile robot workspace include in the lecture’s definition?', ['Attainable positions and orientations within the environment','Only the physical volume of the chassis body','Only the set of motor current values','Every drawn point in the environment, regardless of obstacles or constraints'], ['Workspace is a set of reachable poses, not merely occupied body shape.','Body geometry is not the same as the range of attainable poses.','Current values are actuator inputs, not poses.','The surrounding environment is not automatically entirely reachable and collision-free.'],kind='diagram')
c=concept(602,'Mobile manipulation and gross/fine motion','Arm-on-base architecture, expanded task reach and the illustrated motion division.')
ask(c,'What arrangement does the mobile-manipulation illustration show?', ['A manipulator arm and gripper mounted on a wheeled base','A fixed-base arm with no locomotion mechanism','A Stewart platform used as the only drive system','A bare mobile chassis with no arm'], ['The drawing combines an articulated arm with a mobile chassis.','Visible wheels and the base distinguish it from the fixed-arm case.','The picture is not a six-leg parallel platform.','The arm and end effector are clearly shown.'],kind='diagram')
ask(c,'Which gross-versus-fine motion division is emphasized?', ['The base supplies gross relocation while the arm performs fine tasks','The base must perform every fine grasp motion while the arm stays locked','Gross and fine motion must both be performed only by the wrist sensor','The arm’s motion eliminates every need to reposition the base'], ['This combines large-area mobility with local manipulation.','That reverses the source’s stated useful division.','A sensor does not replace actuated base and arm motions.','The base is included specifically to extend relocation capability.'])
ask(c,'Does mounting an arm on a mobile base imply that every environmental pose is now reachable?', ['No; base access, obstacles, arm limits and stability still constrain reachable tasks','Yes; mobility removes every workspace restriction','Yes; the arm no longer has joint limits','No; the base cannot change any reachable tool position'], ['Mobility expands the set of possible tasks but does not remove physical constraints.','Expansion is not universality.','The arm’s physical joint limits remain.','Moving the base changes where the arm can operate.'],difficulty='medium',pages=[602,603,601])
c=concept(603,'Integrated autonomous mobile design','Unique summary modules, combined configuration planning and coupled design constraints.')
ask(c,'Which five competence areas are explicitly listed for autonomous mobility in the summary?', ['Locomotion, perception, localization, cognition and navigation','Locomotion, commutation, gear meshing, winding cooling and bearing lubrication','Perception, calibration, painting, packaging and shipping','Localization, machining, casting, welding and fastening'], ['These are the five named autonomy modules.','These are mainly mechanical/electrical implementation concerns, not the summary’s five competence areas.','These do not match the listed autonomy functions.','Manufacturing operations are not the specified competence list.'])
ask(c,'Why does mobile-manipulator path planning need to consider the combined configuration rather than only a collision-free base center?', ['The arm can collide or destabilize the system even when the base center path is clear','The arm has no geometry once the base starts moving','A clear base-center point proves every attached link clears every obstacle','The combined configuration has no relationship to stability'], ['The summary calls for configuration-space planning and stability management of the whole system.','The arm’s links remain physical bodies.','A point-path check cannot certify clearance of the extended robot.','Arm configuration changes load placement and can affect stability.'],difficulty='medium',kind='scenario',assumptions=['The arm may change configuration and extends beyond the chassis footprint.'])
ask(c,'Which design approach matches the summary’s integrated robot-design statement?', ['Co-design actuation, transmission, sensing, stiffness and control','Choose only the controller and assume all mechanical compliance is negligible','Choose only the motor’s peak torque and ignore the transmission and sensors','Choose only the base shape and postpone all stability checks indefinitely'], ['The lecture emphasizes interaction among these subsystems.','A controller-only choice omits the stiffness and sensing issues discussed.','Motor rating alone does not capture drive losses, compliance and feedback.','Base geometry and stability must be considered with the remaining system.'])
